    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so earlier misuse is not ruled out, and the burst itself is not yet established as misuse.\n- **A2: Yes.** The burst (new proxy, new fingerprint, payout changes) happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes without flagging. This assumes the check could see request and account-change data, which the case does not state.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link, but it could also reflect a shared processor, an agent, or a common mule account.\n- **A4: Yes, weakly.** Thirty days is long enough that a diverted payout would often have been reported, so silence is some evidence, but far from proof.\n- **A5: Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout changes, is worth investigating whatever the outcome.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1: Yes.** D has 0 chargebacks in 100 transactions against 2 in 100 for each of the others, on equal denominators.\n- **B2: No.** A combined figure says nothing about how events are spread; one account could be well above 5 percent.\n- **B3: Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. It shows this for that identity only; accounts under a nominee would not appear.\n- **B4: Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; the second may have read the first note, or both may rest on the same model output.\n- **B5: No.** A blocked payment cannot generate a chargeback, so the absence of one says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates, a shared descriptor pattern and common settlement accounts are enough to justify looking at the four together.\n\nRESULT CF-055 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Yes | A3: No, not by itself | A4: Yes, weakly | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: Yes | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
